11.5 Why the network needs a non-linearity
41:06 A student asks why these particular functions, and Katanforoosh answers by taking the activation away. With the identity in every neuron of the 3-2-1 network the forward pass substitutes into itself:

\hat y = W^{[3]}\Bigl(W^{[2]}\bigl(W^{[1]}x + b^{[1]}\bigr) + b^{[2]}\Bigr) + b^{[3]}
= \underbrace{W^{[3]}W^{[2]}W^{[1]}}_{W}\,x + \underbrace{W^{[3]}W^{[2]}b^{[1]} + W^{[3]}b^{[2]} + b^{[3]}}_{b}.
\tag{11.4}
43:52 Three layers of parameters compute exactly what one layer computes, an affine function Wx + b, and adding layers changes nothing: however deep, the network is a linear regression. The capacity of a network to represent complicated functions comes entirely from its activations. The lecture’s phrasing is that any function but the identity will do; strictly, the problem is any affine activation, since g(z) = cz + d collapses in the same way, and what is needed is a non-linear one. The generator confirms the collapse on random inputs to machine precision.

11.9 Mini-batch gradient descent
1:05:16 The two versions of gradient descent seen so far sit at opposite extremes. Batch gradient descent vectorises well but makes one update per pass through the data, and with a million images one update is very slow. Stochastic gradient descent updates after every example, quickly but noisily, and gives up vectorisation. Mini-batch gradient descent takes the middle. Split the training set into batches, written with curly braces, X^{\{1\}}, \dots, X^{\{T\}}, of 1,000 examples each, and loop:

pick a batch \bigl(X^{\{t\}}, Y^{\{t\}}\bigr);
forward-propagate the whole batch and compute its cost J^{\{t\}} = \frac{1}{1000} \sum_{i} L\bigl(\hat y^{(i)}, y^{(i)}\bigr);
backpropagate to get the gradients of J^{\{t\}};
update W^{[l]} and b^{[l]} for every layer.

Each update is a matrix computation over a thousand examples, which a GPU does at once, and a thousand examples already give a useful estimate of the direction to move in. 1:09:30 The price is noise. Plotted against iterations, the cost of batch gradient descent falls smoothly, while that of mini-batch descent falls on average but wobbles, since each step follows one batch’s estimate of the gradient. The smaller the batch, the larger the wobble, and quantitatively: for batches drawn at random, the variance of the gradient estimate is inversely proportional to the batch size. On a toy regression the generator measures 9.9, 0.98 and 0.10 for batches of 1, 10 and 100.

16.7 Markov decision processes
56:29 Reinforcement learning problems are modelled as Markov decision processes. An MDP is a five-tuple (S, A, \{P_{sa}\}, \gamma, R):

S, the set of states: chess positions, or the helicopter’s possible positions, orientations and velocities;
A, the set of actions: legal moves, or stick positions;
P_{sa}, the state transition probabilities: for each state s and action a, a distribution over the next state;
\gamma \in [0, 1), the discount factor, explained in Section 16.8;
R : S \to \mathbb{R}, the reward function.

58:29 The running example is small enough to draw. Ng takes it from Russell and Norvig’s textbook (Figure 16.4). A robot moves on a 4 \times 3 grid with one cell blocked by a pillar, so there are 11 states, written (x, y) from the bottom left. The actions are the four compass directions. Real robots’ wheels slip, so the model makes moves noisy. A command goes in the intended direction with probability 0.8 and veers to each side with probability 0.1. From (3, 1), a command to go North reaches (3, 2) with probability 0.8, (2, 1) with 0.1 and (4, 1) with 0.1. A move into a wall or the pillar leaves the robot where it is. Ng stresses that this is a caricature of real robot dynamics, chosen so that the whole MDP fits on the board.
1:02:41 The task is specified entirely through the reward. Ng puts R = +1 at (4, 3), the goal, and R = -1 at (4, 2), a cell to avoid. To make the robot hurry, every other state carries a small negative reward, R(s) = -0.02, which Ng describes as charging the robot for the electricity it spends loitering. Lecture 17 adds that the \pm 1 cells are absorbing: once the robot reaches one, it collects that reward and the world ends.






Figure 16.4: Left: the gridworld’s rewards and its transition model. The pillar is grey, the absorbing \pm 1 cells are shaded, and every other cell costs 0.02 per visit. From (3, 1) a North command succeeds with probability 0.8 and slips sideways with probability 0.1 each way. Right: the optimal policy for R = -0.02 and \gamma = 0.99. From (3, 1) it goes West, the long way round.
